        "totalCardsInTopic": 3,
        "title": "Salary Components Breakdown",
        "content": "Let us now do a post-mortem of every component on your salary slip. Understanding the business rationale, tax impact, and ideal ratio of each allowance will help you save tax and negotiate smartly with HR at the next job switch.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.1 Basic Salary — The Anchor of Your Paycheck\n\n**Basic Salary** is the foundational core of your total earnings. All other components (HRA, EPF, Gratuity, Leave Encashment, Overtime) are calculated as a percentage (%) of the basic salary.\n\n#### Golden Rule of Basic Salary Ratio:\n> **Ideal Basic Salary = 40% to 50% of total CTC.**\n\nWhy is this ratio critical?\n\n#### Scenario Analysis: Comparative Impact of Basic Salary Ratios on a Rs.10 LPA CTC\n\nLet us compare three different corporate pay structures for the exact same **Rs.10,00,000 CTC package**:\n\n#### Table 16.3: Impact of Basic Salary Percentage on Take-Home and Benefits (Rs.10 LPA CTC)\n\n| Parameter | Low Basic Structure (30% Basic) | Ideal Basic Structure (50% Basic) | High Basic Structure (70% Basic) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Annual Basic Salary** | Rs.3,00,000 (Rs.25,000/mo) | Rs.5,00,000 (Rs.41,667/mo) | Rs.7,00,000 (Rs.58,333/mo) |\n| **Annual HRA (50% of Basic)** | Rs.1,50,000 (Rs.12,500/mo) | Rs.2,50,000 (Rs.20,833/mo) | Rs.3,50,000 (Rs.29,167/mo) |\n| **Employer EPF (12% of Basic)** | Rs.36,000 (Rs.3,000/mo) | Rs.60,000 (Rs.5,000/mo) | Rs.84,000 (Rs.7,000/mo) |\n| **Gratuity Pool (4.81% Basic)** | Rs.14,430 (Rs.1,203/mo) | Rs.24,050 (Rs.2,004/mo) | Rs.33,670 (Rs.2,806/mo) |\n| **Special Allowance (Balance)** | Rs.4,99,570 (Rs.41,631/mo) | Rs.1,65,950 (Rs.13,829/mo) | Rs.0 |\n| **Monthly In-Hand (Approx)** | **~Rs.68,500 / month** | **~Rs.64,200 / month** | **~Rs.59,800 / month** |\n| **Maximum HRA Tax Exemption** | Low Exemption Cap (Rs.1.5L) | High Exemption Cap (Rs.2.5L) | Very High Cap (Rs.3.5L) |\n| **Retirement Savings Rate** | Slow Wealth Building | Balanced Wealth Building | Aggressive Wealth Building |\n\n#### Key Lessons from Table 16.3:\n- **Low Basic (30%):** You get the HIGHEST take-home cash today but sacrifice retirement wealth and HRA tax exemption.\n- **Ideal Basic (50%):** Balanced approach — decent take-home, good HRA exemption room, and healthy EPF/Gratuity accumulation.\n- **High Basic (70%):** LOWEST take-home but maximum retirement savings and HRA tax exemption. Best for those who rent expensive houses in metros.\n\n---\n\n### 16.2.2 HRA (House Rent Allowance) — Tax Exemption Masterclass\n\nHRA is one of the most powerful tax-saving tools in your salary structure, but only if you know how to claim it correctly.\n\n#### HRA Exemption Formula (Section 10(13A)):\nThe HRA exemption is the MINIMUM of these 3 rules:\n1. **Actual HRA Received** from employer.\n2. **Rent Paid minus 10% of Basic Salary**.\n3. **50% of Basic Salary** (for Metro cities: Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai) or **40% of Basic Salary** (for non-Metro cities).",
        "color": "#10B981",
        "emoji": "💼",
        "interactiveType": "no"
